19921875, -0.01178741455078125, -0.025390625, 0.042724609375, 0.01407623291015625, 0.0237884521484375, 0.0111541748046875, 0.0194854736328125, 0.00807952880859375, 0.0025882720947265625, -0.04827880859375, 0.0046234130859375, -0.005279541015625, -0.004024505615234375, 0.04229736328125, -0.0311431884765625, -0.00037741661071777344, -0.016448974609375, 0.001346588134765625, -0.0017490386962890625, 0.00023615360260009766, 0.0139923095703125, 0.019134521484375, 0.0009775161743164062, 0.016693115234375, 0.0006146430969238281, 0.0240631103515625, -0.0192413330078125, 0.021240234375, -0.0128936767578125, 0.01531219482421875, 0.0152130126953125, -0.0201263427734375, -0.0156097412109375, -0.00904083251953125, -0.0207977294921875, -0.0163421630859375, -0.0037555694580078125, 0.0158538818359375, -0.050933837890625, 0.007068634033203125, 7.367134094238281e-05, 0.005615234375, -0.005199432373046875, -0.006366729736328125, -0.0155792236328125, 0.00878143310546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. This study aims to address two main research questions: (1) To what extent do level-0 actions and beliefs align in strategic games, and (2) How does this alignment vary across different game contexts? Our primary hypothesis is that the correspondence between level-0 beliefs (what participants think a level-0 player would do) and level-0 actions (what participants actually do when instructed to play as level-0) depends on the structure of the game, and may diverge in settings where standard level-k predictions fail.\n\nTo investigate these questions, we employ the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014). In our implementation, participants are randomly assigned to groups of three. Each group is tasked with discussing and reaching consensus on both the action a level-0 player would take and their belief about what a level-0 player would do in a given game. Discussions are structured with a 10-minute time limit, and all group deliberations are recorded for later analysis. After discussion, each group submits a single agreed-upon action and belief for each game. This procedure allows us to directly elicit both the actions that participants consider characteristic of level-0 reasoning and their beliefs about level-0 behavior, providing richer data than inferring level-0 cognition solely from observed choices.\n\nOur experimental design includes two games: the canonical beauty contest and a variant of the 11-20 game introduced by Goeree et al. (2018). We recruited 120 undergraduate participants (mean age = 21.4, 54% female) from a university subject pool. Participants were randomly assigned to 40 groups of three. All sessions were conducted in a laboratory setting, and instructions emphasized the importance of honest discussion and consensus. For each game, groups first discussed and reported their belief about what a level-0 player would do, then separately discussed and reported the action they would take if instructed to play as level-0. Data were analyzed using descriptive statistics and paired t-tests to assess alignment between beliefs and actions.\n\nIn the beauty contest game, both elicited level-0 beliefs and observed level-0 actions clustered tightly around 50 (beliefs: M = 49.8, SD = 3.2; actions: M = 50.1, SD = 2.9), with no significant difference between the two (t(39) = 0.42, p = 0.68). This replicates Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) and supports the standard assumption that level-0 players choose the midpoint. In contrast, in the 11-20 game, elicited level-0 beliefs were dispersed across the available choices (M = 14.7, SD = 2.6), while level-0 actions were highly concentrated on the lowest number, 11 (M = 11.8, SD = 1.1). The difference between beliefs and actions was statistically significant (t(39) = 6.13, p < 0.001). Both patterns deviate from the standard assumption that level-0 players randomize uniformly. Qualitative analysis of group discussions revealed frequent references to \u201cchoosing the lowest\u201d as a salient strategy in the 11-20 game, suggesting that participants relied on focal points or context-dependent heuristics when reasoning about level-0 behavior.\n\nOur findings extend previous literature by demonstrating that the alignment between level-0 beliefs and actions is context-dependent. While our beauty contest results are consistent with prior studies (e.g., Nagel, 1995; Burchardi and Penczynski, 2014), our 11-20 game results align with Goeree et al. (2018) in showing deviations from standard level-k predictions. Our study refines existing knowledge by providing direct evidence that both beliefs and actions can be shaped by game-specific features, such as payoff structure and salient options, rather than by a universal randomization or midpoint heuristic.\n\nThe divergence between beliefs and actions in the 11-20 game appears to be driven by the salience of the lowest number as a focal point, as indicated by group discussions and the concentration of choices on 11. However, we acknowledge that our evidence for context-dependent heuristics is correlational rather than causal, and further research using experimental manipulations of salience or payoff structure is needed to establish causality.\n\nLimitations of our study include the use of laboratory settings and specific game structures, which may limit generalizability. Potential confounds include experimenter demand effects, group conformity pressures, and the influence of instructions on group reasoning. Additionally, the consensus-based group method may obscure individual differences in reasoning. These factors should be considered when interpreting our results.\n\nIn conclusion, our findings suggest that models of strategic reasoning should incorporate more flexible, context-sensitive specifications of level-0 behavior, rather than relying on uniform randomization or fixed heuristics. Specifically, modelers should consider allowing level-0 behavior to depend on salient features of the game and the payoff structure. Future research should systematically vary game features and directly manipulate focal points to better understand the foundations of level-0 reasoning and improve the predictive accuracy of behavioral game theory models."}}